20:# Checking tensor rewrites in IR Workbench
24:Stage 1 produced an executable tensor IR, two evaluation modes and two checked rewrites in TypeScript. This stage runs on the host. CUDA lowering belongs to a later stage. An independent evaluator tested revision `8b94514`. All 25 hidden acceptance tests passed, and the f32 evaluator agreed bit-for-bit with the independent reference on 180 program/input pairs. Our own tests also exercised a rejection. On one row of eight numbers, the two reduction orders gave adjacent f32 results. The very same candidate failed the exact check and passed the tolerance check.
26:## From indices to a checked program
31:fn matmul(a: f32[M,K], b: f32[K,N]): f32[M,N] {
32:  c[i,j] = sum(k) a[i,k] * b[k,j];
33:  return c;
37:The plain subscript `i` on the first axis of `a` gives `i` extent `M`. The second axis of `b` gives `j` extent `N`. Both operands supply the same extent `K` for the reduction index. The compiler checks those equalities symbolically, so two unrelated shape names would be a source error even if one caller happened to pass equal sizes. Each shape name has a bare axis in a parameter type from which to obtain its value at a call. Other dimensions can be affine expressions of those names. Parameters and results have at least one axis, and an axis can have extent zero. Calls use finite input values and consistent concrete shapes, with data laid out in row-major order.
43:## An IR with explicit iteration order
47:I chose this level because both passes need information already present in index notation. To inline, the compiler needs a producer body and the mapping from its axes to a consumer's subscripts. To split a sum, it needs the extent, iteration order and point at which accumulation starts. A control-flow graph of loads, branches and increments would require the passes to reconstruct these facts. Retaining source text would make each pass parse them again. The indexed IR keeps them directly executable. For a blocked sum, the inner axis records which outer block controls its range.
53:## Two precisions on the same loops
59:The f64 mode uses JavaScript doubles throughout and stores intermediates and outputs in `Float64Array`. Its job in the optimizer is to evaluate the original expression on the same f32 samples supplied to the candidate. The two modes share traversal and address calculation. An indexing bug could therefore make them agree on an incorrect element. Independent expected tensors for the corpus and an independent evaluator for the random programs check that part of the implementation. [Evaluator](../../../../../../src/tensor/evaluate.ts) · [Language and precision tests](../../../../../../tests/tensor/language.test.mjs)
61:## Two passes, two promises
69:## What the meaning check observes
97:## What changed in the framework
102:if (!candidate.changed) return candidate;
103:const check = synchronous(verify(before, candidate.artifact), 'Native pass verifier');
104:Check.validate(check);
111:## Measurements and independent evaluation
113:The six corpus programs have small numerical oracles. Rectangular matmul checks axis order. Max-shifted softmax runs on logits around 1,000, where direct exponentiation would overflow. Layer normalization uses distinct gamma and beta values and tests both constant and nonconstant rows with explicit epsilon. Single-head attention has a uniform row and a row sensitive to the dot-product scaling. The two-layer MLP checks tanh-GELU between affine layers. The NCHW convolution uses sparse asymmetric kernel taps across two channels and two batches, so a tap reversal or a wrong stride selects a different pixel. Each oracle runs before and after the pass sequence in both precisions. [Corpus checks](../../../../../../tests/tensor/corpus.test.mjs)
117:The implementation build passed on Node 24.18.0, along with 19 tensor tests, all 18 framework test files and 10 affected Typed test files. For this report I rebuilt the evaluated revision, reran its tensor tests and measured the corpus in pass order `inline`, then `split-sum`. All 12 steps were accepted. Inline changed attention and the MLP. Split-sum changed all five programs with eligible reductions. Convolution's one sum binds three indices, so both passes left it intact. For the five changed split results, I compared final f32 output with origin f64 output on the actual contract schedule. Across 60 shape/fill cases and 2,415 elements, the largest error as a fraction of the allowed error was 0.02983, about 3%. It occurred at flat element 21 in the MLP with all extents 8 and signed inputs. That is a retrospective measurement on this corpus, separate from the independent evaluation.
119:The independent evaluation used revision `8b94514`. The hidden suite was written from the fixed language and API brief before implementation. A separate differential cross-check generated 60 fixed-core programs and ran three inputs per program against an independent reference.
121:| Independent check | Reported outcome |
122:| --- | --- |
123:| Hidden acceptance suite | 25 of 25 tests passed. |
124:| Original programs, f32 | 180 of 180 evaluations matched the reference bit-for-bit. |
125:| Original programs, f64 | 180 of 180 were within 10⁻⁹ relative to max(1, ∣r∣). |
126:| Inline on the same 60 programs | 60 accepted. Every accepted result was bit-identical to the original on the tested inputs. |
127:| Split-sum on the same 60 programs | 60 accepted. Every accepted result agreed in f64 within the reported 10⁻⁹ criterion. |
129:The independent evaluation reported zero failures and zero rejections. Its f64 cross-check and the optimizer's mixed-precision tolerance answer different questions. The former checks agreement with an independent implementation. The latter decides whether a particular reassociation remains close enough when executed in f32.
131:## What I would do differently
133:I would record how many pass applications changed an artifact alongside how many were accepted. Both counts are already accessible internally. On the six corpus programs, the public result alone reads as twelve successes. Comparing adjacent revision hashes identifies the seven steps that actually rewrote code. The independent results establish acceptance on all 60 programs for each pass. Their summary gives no count of changed revisions. Recording both counts would make rewrite coverage visible while keeping the three-function API intact.
135:I would also keep the largest observed fraction of the tolerance with each successful test run, alongside the exact bits for the rounding example. A pass/fail result tells the compiler whether to adopt a candidate. The fraction tells a reader how close that decision came to its threshold on the cases actually run. The local MLP measurement could be recovered from the existing sampler and evaluators after the fact. Collecting it at verification time would have given the original tolerance choice an empirical record from the first corpus run.
137:I would make the positive input domain for epsilon explicit when specifying the layer-normalization cases. The current sampler identifies it by shape alone. Consequently every fixed one-element parameter receives the same three positive samples, even if it represents an ordinary signed coefficient. The language handles signed coefficients, but those samples exercise only their positive values. Giving epsilon its own case specification would let other one-element parameters receive signed inputs too.

